\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Concepts behind Bayesian Networks}

A Bayesian network (BN) is a probabilistic graphical model that represents a set of variables and their conditional dependencies via a directed acyclic graph (DAG). The DAG is used to capture the relationships and knowledge about the interdependencies of these variables.

Bayesian networks are used to determine the joint probability distribution \(p(\mathbf{X})\) of a set of random variables \(\mathbf{X}\) by using conditional probability and applying the chain rule:

\begin{align}
p(\mathbf{X}) = p(X_1) \prod_{k=2}^{n}p(X_k|X_{k-1},....,X_1)
\end{align}

Where \(p(X_k|X_{k-1},....,X_1)\) represents the conditional probability of node \(X_k\) given its parents \(X_{k-1},....,X_1 \) in the DAG.

Each node in the network is associated with a probability distribution table, also known as its CPT or conditional probability table. The CPT provides the conditional probability values for each child variable given the combination of values for its parent variables.

One of the advantages of using Bayesian networks is that they allow the propagation of information from the variables with known values to the variables with uncertain values. This feature makes them suitable for reasoning under uncertainty and decision making in complex systems.

In this book, we will discussing various examples and techniques for constructing Bayesian networks and performing probabilistic inference with them.

